<h1>Who audits your site</h1>
<p class="lede">I'm María Pía Peña Foissac, and I do the audits at tuaccesible.</p>

<div class="prose">
  <img class="portrait" src="/img/maria-pia.jpg" width="1458" height="1534" alt="">

  <p>I've used a screen reader for more than thirteen years. It isn't a tool I open in order to work: it's my primary way of using a computer, every day, inside and outside this consultancy. When I test your product with NVDA, I'm using it the same way I use everything else.</p>
  <p>I work with NVDA, JAWS, Windows Narrator, and TalkBack on Android, and with keyboard-only navigation. That's the part of the job where the barriers no automated tool flags actually show up.</p>

  <h2>What I audit, and since when</h2>
  <p>I've audited accessibility professionally since 2021: more than four years evaluating websites, mobile apps, documents, and desktop software against WCAG 2.2, EN 301 549, and the European Accessibility Act.</p>
  <p>I write accessibility review reports with findings prioritized by severity, accessibility statements, and cognitive accessibility recommendations. My background is in software QA, so a finding reaches your team with steps to reproduce it and an acceptance criterion, not as a loose observation.</p>

  <h2>I also teach</h2>
  <p>I teach digital accessibility at the DALAT Foundation, and I delivered the accessibility module for XAcademy. Teaching forced me to explain technical barriers to people who don't write code, and that changed how I write reports: if a content owner can't understand the finding, the finding doesn't get fixed.</p>

  <h2>Certifications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A11Y Development Knowledge Badge — IAAP, January 2026.</li>
    <li>Digital Accessibility Specialist, WCAG 2.2 Level AA — Instituto de Accesibilidad, May 2025.</li>
    <li>ARIA from Scratch for Developers and Designers — WeAAAre, July 2025.</li>
    <li>Digital Accessibility — Universidad Tecnológica Nacional, 2020.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>I don't work alone</h2>
  <p>I do the auditing, but the report you receive went through another reading before it left. That second review checks the findings and the prioritization, and it's the reason we deliver a little later than one person working alone would.</p>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>Let's talk</h2>
  <p>If you have a product to audit, tell me where it stands and on what timeline. I'll tell you where to start.</p>
